  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 11 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-sort-basic" questions={quiz} />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A sorting algorithm puts values in order. We count its work in comparisons and moves (swaps or shifts).</li>
          <li>A swap needs a helper variable, <code>temp</code>, or one value is lost.</li>
          <li><b>Bubble sort</b>: neighbors swap; each pass moves the biggest to the end. <b>Selection sort</b>: find the smallest, swap it to the front. <b>Insertion sort</b>: shift bigger values right, insert the key.</li>
          <li>All three need about n × n / 2 comparisons in the worst case. Insertion sort is fast on almost-sorted data; selection sort makes the fewest swaps.</li>
          <li>Bubble and insertion sort are <b>stable</b> (ties keep their order); selection sort is not.</li>
          <li>In real programs, use <code>Arrays.sort</code>.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next lesson:</b> Advanced sorting: merge sort, quick sort, and heap sort (coming soon). After that: <a href={base + '/itc120/searching/'}>Linear and binary search</a>, where sorted data pays off.</p>
      <div class="refs small muted">
